WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:04.790
This video introduces Sikuli,
an interactive technique

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.490
that uses screenshots
to search and automate

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.010
graphical user interfaces.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:14.180
Sikuli Search allows users to
search a documentation database

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.440
using screenshots of interface
elements, like this dialog box.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.080
User draws a rectangle
around the dialog box

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.840
to capture it's screenshot
and submit it to Sikuli.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:24.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.230
Sikuli currently
searches a database

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:32.810
of more than 50,000 screenshots
from over 100 computer books.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.230
It then returns a list of pages
containing the screenshots

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.030
of the same dialog box.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.220
User can browse
and select a page

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.610
to read the details
about this dialog box.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.390
Sikuli Script allows users
to write simple scripts

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.390
to automate GUI
interaction by screenshots.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.730
Consider this bus
tracking interface.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.630
With Sikuli Script, a user can
draw a rectangle around the bus

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:00.590
symbol to take it screenshot
and select a neighborhood

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.170
on the map to watch
for the bus arrival,

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.480
also by taking a screenshot.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.880
After taking the
screenshots, the user

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.000
can use them directly in
a statement asking Sikuli

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.220
to find the
neighborhood on the map,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.040
look inside, and
find the bus symbol.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.800
If such a symbol is
found, a pop-up window

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.810
can be displayed to notify
the user the bus has arrived.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.130
Sikuli Script supports
many visual commands.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.710
It can be used to click
on visual targets,

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:37.480
to drag a visual target onto
another visual target, to type

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.870
a string in a visual target,
in this case, the Google Search

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:42.140
box in the web browser.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:49.000
With Sikuli, what you
see is what you search

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.930
and what you see
is what you script.

